  "content": {
    "title": "‘Bachelorette’ Season With Taylor Frankie Paul Canceled After Assault Video",
    "text": "AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENTTaylor Frankie Paul, who was set to star in the TV show, had pleaded guilty to aggravated assault after an encounter with her partner in 2023.Listen · 6:26 min Taylor Frankie Paul, right, in a preview for her season of “The Bachelorette.”Credit...John Fleenor/DisneyMarch 19, 2026An upcoming season of “The Bachelorette” was pulled on Thursday, a spokesperson for Disney said, after a video of its star attacking the father of one of her children was leaked.Taylor Frankie Paul, a reality star who was to lead the new season slated to premiere on Sunday, has been under scrutiny in recent days after it emerged that she was facing a domestic violence investigation, years after pleading guilty to aggravated assault in a separate encounter.Disney’s ABC, which broadcasts “The Bachelorette,” had appeared to be committed to continuing its plans to air the show. But on Thursday, TMZ published leaked footage of a physical altercation from 2023 in which Ms. Paul, who rose to fame in the reality series “The Secret Lives of Mormon Wives,” is shown putting her partner in a headlock and throwing metal chairs at him.“In light of the newly released video just surfaced today, we have made the decision to not move forward with the new season of ‘The Bachelorette’ at this time,” the Disney spokesperson said in a statement, “and our focus is on supporting the family.”“The Bachelor” franchise has dealt with its share of controversy in the past, but this is the most significant fallout in the show’s history.Through a spokesperson, Ms. Paul said she is “very grateful for ABC’s support as she prioritizes her family’s safety and security.” The statement added: “She is currently exploring all of her options, seeking support, and preparing to own and share her story.”Subscribe to The Times to read as many articles as you like.Julia Jacobs is an arts and culture reporter who often covers legal issues for The Times.John Koblin covers the television industry for The Times.A version of this article appears in print on March 21, 2026, Section C, Page 4 of the New York edition with the headline: A Video Derails ‘The Bachelorette’. Order Reprints | Today’s Paper | SubscribeAdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
